**Quick answer:** To get independent interview feedback, decide the competencies before interviewing, give each interviewer a scorecard, and don't let them see anyone else's rating until they've submitted their own. On GetHired you build an interview plan for each job, put one to six people on each interview, and every interviewer gets a kit and a scorecard. Reviews are blind in a set window, submitted ratings are locked, and the decision stays with your team.

The usual debrief goes wrong in a predictable way. The most senior or fastest person says "I liked them", and everyone else adjusts. You paid for four opinions and got one.

## What is an interview scorecard?

An interview scorecard is a short form each interviewer fills in about one candidate: a rating for each competency, notes with evidence, and an overall recommendation. It makes interviewers judge the same things and gives you a record to compare.

On GetHired:

{% table %}
-
- Part
- Details
---
- Competency ratings
- Strong no, No, Yes or Strong yes for each. Optional, so interviewers skip what they didn't assess
---
- Competency notes
- The evidence behind each rating
---
- Overall recommendation
- Required to submit
---
- Overall note
- Anything that doesn't fit a competency
{% /table %}

There are four ratings and no neutral middle, so each interviewer has to lean one way.

## How do you set up an interview plan?

An interview plan lists the rounds for a job. For each interview you set:

- a **name**, such as "Technical interview";
- a **length**, from 15 to 240 minutes;
- the **competencies** it covers;
- suggested **questions** and instructions;
- default **interviewers**.

A plan can hold up to 20 interviews, though most small teams need far fewer. You can copy a plan from another job, or ask the [recruiter agent](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/meet-the-gethired-recruiter-agent-hire-tech-talent-from-one-chat) to draft one. Plan text is checked for fairness, so it doesn't ask about protected characteristics.

Changing the plan later doesn't change interviews already on the books. Each keeps the competencies it was created with, so its scorecards stay consistent.

## What should each interviewer cover?

Split the competencies instead of having everyone assess everything. For a software engineer it might look like this:

{% table %}
-
- Interview
- Competencies
---
- Hiring manager
- Ownership, role motivation
---
- Technical
- Problem solving, debugging
---
- System design
- Trade-offs, technical communication
---
- Team
- Collaboration, giving and taking feedback
{% /table %}

Our [structured interviews guide](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/structured-interviews-for-tech-hiring-how-to-build-scorecards-and-rubrics) explains how to write a rubric for each one.

## How do interviewers get what they need?

Everyone on an interview's panel gets an **interview kit**: the job, the candidate's resume and application answers, the instructions, the competencies with suggested questions, and their own scorecard.

Being on the panel is what grants the kit. An interviewer doesn't need access to your whole applicant list to do their one interview. You can also record an interview you arranged elsewhere, so its panel gets the same kit and scorecards.

## How does blind interview feedback work?

While you're on the panel for an application and still owe a scorecard, you don't see other interviewers' scorecards, or the ratings on their notes. This applies while your interview is upcoming and for 7 days after it ends. Submitting your own scorecard ends it for you.

The rule is enforced in the database, not just hidden on screen. Note text, the application timeline apart from scorecard recommendations, and the AI screening report stay visible.

When you submit, your competency ratings and overall recommendation are **locked for good**, because you can now see everyone else's. You can still correct your written notes for 24 hours, and edits are recorded.

## How do you get scorecards submitted on time?

- When an interview is completed, interviewers who haven't submitted get an email with a link to their kit.
- If it's still missing after 24 hours, they get one reminder.
- Overdue scorecards show on your [hiring dashboard](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/recruiting-dashboard-what-to-check-every-morning), and you can remind the panel.
- Pipeline board cards show how much feedback is in.

Ask interviewers to write notes soon after the interview. Details fade quickly, and vague notes are hard to compare.

## How do you run the debrief?

1. **Wait for the scorecards.** Don't discuss the candidate before they're in.
2. **Start with disagreements.** Where two people rated the same competency differently, ask each for their evidence.
3. **Separate evidence from impression.** "Walked through how they traced the memory leak" counts. "Seems sharp" doesn't, on its own.
4. **Decide against the role's competencies,** not against the last candidate you met.

Once at least two scorecards are in, someone with permission to view all scorecards can ask for an **AI summary** of agreements, disagreements by competency and open concerns. It quotes only scorecard text, and it never recommends hiring or rejecting.

## Frequently asked questions

### How many interviewers should be on a panel?

Use as few as will cover your competencies. GetHired allows one to six people per interview.

### Can an interviewer change a scorecard after submitting?

Written notes can be edited for 24 hours, with each edit recorded. Competency ratings and the overall recommendation can't be changed.

### Does GetHired average scorecards into a hiring decision?

GetHired doesn't make the decision. It shows each scorecard and a per-competency count of ratings, and your team decides.

### Do candidates see interview scorecards?

No. Candidates never see scorecards or your interview plan's questions.

